[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Hey guys, I\'m Chris from the Silver Symbol Channel, and today we are on my workshop channel, and I\'m going to be sharing a pretty unusual build here out in the middle of the woods. This is my forest outpost, and I\'m going to take you through all the details and share some of the work that went into this project. And we are going to be looking'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'at this build from start to finish, including the structure that I selected, all of the external lighting, and of course the heating system that\'s allowing me to use this thing all year long. All this stuff is worthless if you can\'t power it and of course control it. So, in this video I\'m going to cover all'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the electrical, the wiring, and this control system that is surprisingly cheap, but it can actually cut your wiring time down by about 70%. And you can use these same components in any type of a tiny house, van build, or anything that\'s using 12-V stuff. And of'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'course finally a full tour of the space. It all begins with the structure. This thing is called a Freedom Camper, and these things are designed for four-season camping, kind of like a cooler. Inside, they\'re actually made up of 2 in of solid foam. Then they spray'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'about a 16th of an inch of truck bed liner on the inside and the outside, and that actually makes these things incredibly strong, much cheaper than any other type of camper or a tiny house you could buy. This particular model is'

[01:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'brand new and it\'s 72 in by 89 in. While most of the buyers of these things are putting them in the back of trucks, this larger model is not really going to work that way. You\'ll typically put it on a utility trailer, or in my case, this is going to be a perfect base for'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'my kind of forest outpost that will actually be ready for any season of the year. I definitely did not want to put this on the ground. It wouldn\'t rot out or anything if you did, but I wouldn\'t be able to get the door open if there was 4 ft of snow on the ground. Now, I actually slid this thing on the snow all'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the way to this location, and that was surprisingly easy because it only weighs about 200 lb. But here by myself, I was still able to manage to get it on top of the finished platform. This pressure-treated deck was pretty simple, and I left a lot of space between the'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'boards because I want to make sure any water can drain off. Now, I built this little platform to 8 by 8 because I wanted to have some extra room. One of the secrets to maximizing the space inside a really tiny space is to locate any of your power and other utilities'

[01:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'outside that space. Now, of course this works fine on this platform, but if I was putting these in a truck, I would locate a utility box on either the top or the bottom. But before I do anything else, I\'ve got to secure this camper shell to the deck.'

[02:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And that\'s easy to do. They actually include these plates that simply screw through the bottom into whatever you want to mount it on. You may wonder, why didn\'t I just go ahead and build something like a small cabin? First off, it would have cost a lot more money.'

[02:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Additionally, it wouldn\'t needed to be super insulated if I could actually use it in kind of the real cold months here. The important reason I went this route is because if I get tired of this, I want to move this thing, I actually can. I\'m adding this fully wireless'

[02:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'solar-powered Davis weather station. When you live in the country, it\'s really important to have something like this because the weather reports generally come from airports, and ours is about 2 hours away, and they are never accurate. This thing will solve that problem completely. I also wanted'

[02:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'some exterior light, and this is really important, especially when you\'re in the deep woods. These are designed for 4 by 4. They don\'t use a lot of power, and I\'m even using these rock lights around the base of the structure. These are my favorite, and I think they\'re the most'

[02:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'important, usually used in wheel wells on vehicles, but this red light really gives you a great look. It doesn\'t attract bugs, and at night, if you want to peek out the window, you\'ll be able to see what\'s going on around you, and your eyes won\'t be blinded from that white light. Additionally, you got to'

[03:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'think of kind of the worst weather. If we had a really bad storm, these are even a kind of locator lights. So, if I was outside of this space, I\'d actually be able to find my way back. These lights are also surprisingly affordable. They\'re fully weatherproof, and they simply plug into each other, so they\'re'

[03:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'very easy to wire, and they use 12 V. And you can use this remote control, and there are a million different colors and some crazy modes that I\'m not going to be using. I\'m just going to permanently leave it set to either red or orange. When you\'re in a small space like this,'

[03:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'air flow is super important. Now, the Freedom Camper has a couple of vents on either side that you can\'t really close off. They\'re small, but they\'re designed to allow air and oxygen into the space, which just means you\'re not going to die, but that\'s not enough. When it\'s'

[03:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hot in the summertime, you are going to absolutely want more air flow. So, I\'m using this kind of through-wall ventilation fan. This model is really unique. It\'s easy to install. It also includes a built-in LED light, and it runs on 12 V, and it is very efficient'

[03:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in terms of how much power it\'s going to use. Now, as long as you\'re using a cutting tool, going through these Freedom Campers is pretty easy, but you can punch these things. They don\'t dent. They are super strong. But once I get this piece removed, I\'m able to install the fan, and you get a kind of glimpse'

[04:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'into what this structure is actually made of. My favorite part about these fans are they are super easy to install, and they\'re not picky about how thick your structure is. Here this thing is about 2 and 1/2 in thick, but if you were putting it through a thin-wall camper,'

[04:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they basically can adjust to fit just about any situation you\'ve got. With my heavy-duty silicone put down, I\'m just going to finish up with some stainless steel screws and go around the perimeter of the fan, and now I\'m ready to check it out. The inside of this thing, of'

[04:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'course, includes the LED light, and this fan has two different options. It can either pull air from the rooftop vents downward, but it can\'t move any air unless you open the thing up. That\'s done with this little handle, and it basically pops the top up, and now'

[04:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'ve got access to the outside, and it does include a screen, so bugs are not going to be getting in either way. But the cool thing is, once you open your side windows and you set this thing to exhaust the air, say you\'re sleeping in there, you\'re going to actually feel'

[05:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that nice fresh air getting pulled in from the windows across you, and then it gets exhausted out the roof. Now, we\'re at the part that I think is really the most important, the power, the wiring, how am I tying everything in'

[05:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'together? Because originally when I started this project, I knew I was going to have a fan, lights, and all sorts of different things, but it started to get complicated in how I could actually run them all conveniently. So, of course it all begins with a power source. Here I\'m'

[05:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'using this LiTime lithium iron phosphate battery. Now, this one is big. It\'s 320 amp hours. It is a self-heating battery, and it\'s even got built-in Bluetooth. Now, with this much power, I am going to eventually have to charge it up via'

[05:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'solar, which will be a separate video, but everything here will tie into solar quite nicely. And with 320 amp hours, this thing will actually run this thing for probably 4 months before it needs a recharge. And with the built-in'

[05:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Bluetooth, this is awesome. I can get right into the battery from inside or outside the shell. I can check the voltages and see exactly what percentage of battery time I\'ve got left. Tying everything together was a real challenge, and I kept hunting around for'

[06:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'what I could do or maybe what product could fit the bill, but I didn\'t want to spend a lot. And in this case, I found this product from the company AuxBeam. This is their control panel, and I mounted it all on this PVC or kind of Plexiglas board to give me a good look,'

[06:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and it keeps everything kind of neat and tidy. Most importantly, this is real touch-button control. You can even change the stickers on these buttons to customize it in any way you want. They give you a couple hundred of them. I\'ve also got this USB charging outlet'

[06:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'station. Now, this thing is like 12 bucks and includes two USB-A ports and two USB-Cs, but I like this model cuz you can also shut it off, and it includes a voltmeter that unfortunately just doesn\'t show up on camera, but it\'s crystal clear in person. And this does'

[06:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wire directly to 12 V, and it\'s also on one of those switch loads on the AuxBeam controller. I\'ve also got this hardwired carbon monoxide detector. Now, I wired this in conjunction with my heater, so if anybody\'s using this space and they'

[06:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hit the heater button, they can\'t even switch the monoxide detector off. That\'s going to be really good for safety in the very rare event that somehow some propane or monoxide gets into this small space. Let me give you a quick tour of the wiring and exactly why I went this'

[07:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'route and how it\'s tied into everything. The battery, of course, just has a positive and a negative. The negative runs into this little box here. I\'ll explain in a moment. My positive feeds into this switch. This is a master'

[07:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'disconnect, really cheap and super important cuz if I want to completely kill the power to this space, one switch does it. Pop this cover off. And this little box here'

[07:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is in fact the secret weapon behind this whole setup. So, my battery feeds through the disconnect switch into the bottom of this AuxBeam, and then I get eight connections. Now, I can plug anything I want into these.'

[07:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now, you\'ve got to read the directions and all that, but lights and 12-V appliances are going to work just fine. You wire them into these terminals, and this has got to be the best feature of this control box because I no longer have to run my high-amp power lines all'

[07:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the way back into my trailer or van or whatever I\'ve got. Instead, this little control wire goes inside, and it gets fed into a control panel. And this control panel is a much slicker way to'

[08:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'control anything you want. This other small box here is just a control box for the LED lights that I have on the outside. I\'m just going to use the red, and I'

[08:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'think the red just gives the right amount of nighttime illumination. It\'s easy on your eyes, and if you\'re inside looking out, you just want to see what\'s going on without lighting up the whole area and getting bugs everywhere, I think red\'s going to be the best color. Now, heating this space is critical,'

[08:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'especially if you\'re in a cold climate like me. And if you\'re going to use something like a diesel heater, that is really tricky to use in this type of an installation. You could certainly set a diesel heater outside, but those things are generally not waterproof, so you have to find a way to protect it, and'

[08:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'ve got to deal with a super hot exhaust. And in a very tiny space like this, I really would not recommend any type of an open flame. Even those heater bodies can be kind of risky in such a small space, especially if you\'re going to be sleeping. This is called a Propex'

[08:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'heater. It\'s the PS2000 model. It is certainly quite expensive for what it is, but it\'s easily the most durable heater on the market, and it is actually fired by propane. And this is a completely portable heating system. Everything you need is inside that'

[09:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'weatherproof Pelican case, and you can have your heating system up and running in just about 5 minutes. Propane also gives you a pretty good dry heat. You don\'t get any of that humidity or moisture, and additionally you get this electronic thermostat. Having that is'

[09:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to allow this thing to easily cycle off and on, but keep in mind the thermostat is going to be placed inside the structure. I\'ll be making a full breakout video on this heater cuz there are a lot of details in it, and it\'s a costly item. It does all the heating'

[09:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'inside that Pelican case, but it is a two-hose design. One hose is going to pull the kind of cold air in from the camper shell itself. It gets heated inside this thing, and all the combustion is done externally. Then the'

[09:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'second hose, of course, delivers the heated air into the space. Now, there are a lot of different ways to get those hoses into to structure. If you\'ve got something like a van or a vehicle, you could just simply put the hoses going right through the windows and kind of'

[09:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'seal them up with a towel. But in this case, they made this custom flange. It also allows me to quickly remove it at the end of the winter, which is exactly what I did. Even by the end of this video, you\'ll see that it\'s no longer on the platform. And because I\'m not using any liquid type of fuel like diesel, I'

[10:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can stick this thing in an attic for months or years and nothing\'s going to go bad inside the device. Now, propane is doing the bulk of the heat and that really helps the efficiency, but it does need some electricity. This particular model also has a small battery pack that you can'

[10:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use to power this thing for about 2 weeks. But in my case, once I\'m completed with the install, I\'m actually going to use the external power option, which allows it to be powered directly from my main battery that\'s running the entire camper. And additionally, I\'m'

[10:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to tie it into my control system so that I can even completely turn it off and on from inside the shell. Now, this thing can use everything from small 1-lb propane canisters that could actually heat this thing for about a week right up to a normal gas grill tank'

[10:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and that\'s going to easily heat it for either the entire season or pretty close to it. And this thing seriously is the best feature. This is the electronic thermostat. You can keep it handheld like this, but I will actually mount it on a control panel that I\'m going to put'

[11:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on the wall. But this allows you to set the exact temperature you want. You can kind of forget about it. You can turn it off and on. And it really is a kind of game-changing function that I\'ve never seen on a portable heater like this. Now, we can walk in and I can give you a'

[11:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tour how I have it set up. Now, I confess I do want to put a lot more stuff inside and you could put shelves and cabinets and all sorts of things. This is an ideal space for a little bit of a getaway. If you\'ve got kids, they can easily camp and a two or three kids'

[11:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'could be in this thing or easily two adults. Now, if it looks kind of small on camera, it\'s certainly not a big structure. But when you\'re inside, there\'s plenty of headroom. And if the weather is good, you can certainly use a tent, but this is going to fill in all'

[11:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'those days that aren\'t and that happens a lot, especially here in New Hampshire or if you want to camp in the low temps. So now, when I\'m way out in my woods, if I want to use this space, it\'s sitting right here. My kids can use it or their friends without spending too much money'

[11:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on the structure itself. And I have owned a couple of campers over the years and they all have one thing in common. When you sell them, you almost always lose money. And all those accessories you add generally can\'t be removed. This setup is different. I spent quite a bit of money on some of these parts and'

[12:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pieces, but you can buy as much or as little as you want. And if you decide to get rid of it down the road and things change sometimes, you can easily take them out and hopefully sell or use those accessories elsewhere. And this is definitely something I\'m looking forward'

[12:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to using throughout the summer. And I really can\'t recommend that control system enough. If you\'ve got anything in your life that\'s running on 12-V and you are sick and tired of wiring all those toggle switches underneath a dashboard, consider getting one of those AuxBeam'

[12:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'controllers. It is a total game-changer. And I hope you like this video. If you did, please be sure to give it a thumbs up and subscribe to the Silver Symbol and Silver Symbol Workshop channel for more videos coming up.'
